/**
 * numeric_widens_implicitly — is `from` → `to` an IMPLICIT numeric widening, i.e. a conversion the
 * language performs with no `to` written and with no possibility of a runtime panic?
 *
 * THE RULE (owner ruling 2026-07-29, verbatim): *"Tamanhos menores devem caber em tamanhos maiores e
 * de mesma aridade [...] o mesmo vale para floats e para bigint e decimal. Fazendo pânico somente se
 * ocorrer (em runtime) overflow."* Widening cannot overflow, so this direction never panics; the
 * NARROWING direction has no implicit form at all and must be written as a `to`, which then carries
 * the fail-loud runtime fit guard (`lower_cast_fit_guard`).
 *
 * WHICH LANGUAGE THIS MIRRORS, AND THE DIVERGENCE IT MAKES ON PURPOSE. This is the **C#** shape:
 * implicit widening numeric conversions, plus a checked narrowing that raises on overflow. It is a
 * DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE from Teko's surface reference, **Rust**, which has no implicit numeric
 * widening at all and demands `as` on every width change. The divergence is the owner's call and is
 * named here rather than hidden: nothing in this file claims parity with Rust on numeric conversion.
 *
 * THE ADMITTED SET is exactly the W-RULE's own lossless join (`cast_is_lossless_widen` for the
 * integer pair, the wider IEEE width for the float pair) — deliberately the SAME predicate the
 * ARITHMETIC regime already uses, so an expression and an assignment can never disagree about which
 * widths mix. Before this, `b + a` over `b: i64`/`a: i32` compiled while `b = a` did not, which is
 * the same asymmetry between comparison and arithmetic that the W-RULE was written to end, one
 * regime further along. Mixed int/float stays out (B.38 — an explicit `to` either way).
 *
 * @param from  the value's own type
 * @param to    the target (annotation, parameter, field, or assignment slot) type
 * @return      true iff a value of `from` may become a value of `to` with no cast and no panic
 * @see         cast_is_lossless_widen — the integer half's single source of truth
 * @see         int_arith_join — the arithmetic sibling this keeps in step
 * @since       0.3.1
 */
pub fn numeric_widens_implicitly(from: Type, to: Type) -> bool {
    if type_eq(from, to) { return false }
    if is_integer(from) && is_integer(to) { return cast_is_lossless_widen(prim_kind_of(from), prim_kind_of(to)) }
    if is_float(from) && is_float(to) { return prim_width(prim_kind_of(to)) > prim_width(prim_kind_of(from)) }
    false
}
